<p>
    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Black-green midrange decks lean on
    one-for-one trades (<a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Cabal Therapy">Cabal Therapy</a
    >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Duress"
        >Duress</a
    >, <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Pernicious Deed">Pernicious Deed</a
    >), and that just can't keep up with the card advantage and graveyard
    refueling <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
    > gives you. They don't have a fast clock, so you get plenty of time to
    set up the lock with backup.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> After board, expect graveyard hate
    like <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
    > and color taxing from <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
    >. Cut card-disadvantage filtering like <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
    > for proactive threats (<a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
    >) and permanent removal (<a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
    > and <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
    >). Save your <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
        >Swords to Plowshares</a
    > for their early Wretches—kill those before you start filling your
    graveyard with combo pieces.
</p>
